1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,900
You know the Giza pyramids. But almost nobody knows why Egypt never died after it lost its own kings for 2,300 years.

2
00:00:08,900 --> 00:00:17,100
Persians, Greeks, Romans, Arabs, Turks, the British. Every one of those empires ruled Egypt, and every one of them is gone.

3
00:00:17,100 --> 00:00:24,300
Egypt is still here. The answer is in this picture. By the end of this video, you'll know exactly how it worked.

4
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:32,000
That line is the Nile. Nearly everyone in Egypt lives along it, because almost everything else is desert.

5
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:40,700
Every summer, monsoon rains in the highlands of Ethiopia swelled the river. In Egypt, it flooded the valley for about three months,

6
00:00:40,700 --> 00:00:49,100
and when the water pulled back, it left a layer of black, fertile mud. Egyptians named their country after it: Kemet, the Black Land.

7
00:00:49,100 --> 00:00:58,500
The desert on both sides was Deshret, the Red Land. The Egyptian year was even built around the river. It had three seasons: Flood, Growing and Harvest.

8
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:07,200
Farming, taxes and festivals all followed that cycle. That mud gave Egypt something rare in the ancient world: a reliable surplus.

9
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:16,600
Farmers grew far more grain than they needed, and that extra grain could feed builders, soldiers, priests and officials. It paid for everything you're about to see.

10
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:25,000
They measured the flood on stone stairways called Nilometers. A high flood meant big harvests and higher taxes. A low one meant famine.

11
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:32,400
Keep that in mind: everything in this story rises and falls with the river. The river was also Egypt's highway.

12
00:01:32,400 --> 00:01:41,800
The current carried boats north, and the wind usually blows from the north, so boats could sail back south. Moving grain, stone and armies along the valley was easy.

13
00:01:41,800 --> 00:01:51,200
And the deserts on either side worked like walls. For centuries, they kept large armies out, so Egypt could grow rich without constantly defending itself.

14
00:01:51,200 --> 00:01:58,600
Around 3100 BC, a king from the south conquered the north and united Upper and Lower Egypt.

15
00:01:58,600 --> 00:02:08,300
Upper Egypt was the south, because the Nile flows north. Egyptians remembered the founder as Menes; historians link him to a king called Narmer.

16
00:02:08,300 --> 00:02:17,000
The capital, Memphis, sat where the valley meets the delta, so it could control both. Around the same time, hieroglyphic writing appeared.

17
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:26,400
One of its first big jobs was running the state: recording harvests, taxes and stores, so one king could govern a country hundreds of kilometres long.

18
00:02:26,400 --> 00:02:34,800
Below the king, a chief minister, the vizier, ran the government, and the country was divided into about 40 provinces,

19
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:43,000
each with its own governor. Remember those governors. Egyptians worshipped many gods tied to the world around them: Ra, the sun;

20
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:51,400
Osiris, god of the dead and of rebirth, linked to the flood's yearly return; and his wife, Isis. These gods would last for 3,000 years.

21
00:02:51,400 --> 00:02:59,600
The king wasn't just a ruler. Egyptians believed he was a god on earth, whose job was to keep Ma'at, the order of the universe,

22
00:02:59,600 --> 00:03:06,500
so the sun rose and the river flooded on time. That made him all-powerful, as long as the river behaved.

23
00:03:06,500 --> 00:03:14,700
Egyptians believed the king's spirit lived on after death, and that his afterlife helped keep the whole country in order.

24
00:03:14,700 --> 00:03:21,400
So his tomb mattered to everyone. Early kings were buried under flat mudbrick tombs called mastabas.

25
00:03:21,400 --> 00:03:30,600
Around 2650 BC, King Djoser's official Imhotep built his tomb in stone instead, stacking six layers about 62 metres high.

26
00:03:30,600 --> 00:03:37,300
The Step Pyramid is the oldest large cut-stone building in the world, and it started the age of pyramids.

27
00:03:37,300 --> 00:03:43,500
The next king, Sneferu, struggled to get the shape right. His Bent Pyramid changes angle halfway up,

28
00:03:43,500 --> 00:03:51,400
most likely because the first slope was too steep. His Red Pyramid finally got it right: the first true smooth-sided pyramid.

29
00:03:51,400 --> 00:03:59,100
His son Khufu built the Great Pyramid of Giza around 2560 BC: about 2.3 million stone blocks,

30
00:03:59,100 --> 00:04:07,300
146 metres tall when finished. Nothing built by humans stood taller for about 3,800 years.

31
00:04:07,300 --> 00:04:17,000
Deep inside, narrow passages lead to the king's granite burial chamber. By the time explorers reached it, it was empty. The river made it possible.

32
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:25,200
Most of the stone was cut right at Giza, but fine white limestone for the outer casing came by boat from quarries across the river,

33
00:04:25,200 --> 00:04:31,400
and huge granite beams for the king's burial chamber came from Aswan, hundreds of kilometres upstream.

34
00:04:31,400 --> 00:04:40,100
How they raised the blocks is still debated. The most likely answer is ramps of mudbrick and rubble, with blocks dragged on wooden sledges.

35
00:04:40,100 --> 00:04:48,000
Wetting the sand in front of a sledge cuts the friction sharply, and an Egyptian tomb painting shows a worker doing exactly that.

36
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:56,200
It was not built by slaves. Archaeologists found the builders' town beside the pyramids, with bakeries, breweries and sleeping halls,

37
00:04:56,200 --> 00:05:03,400
and the workers' own tombs nearby. Many were farmers working in rotating shifts, likely during the flood season,

38
00:05:03,400 --> 00:05:10,300
when their fields were under water anyway. We even have a logbook from one of the crews. In 2013,

39
00:05:10,300 --> 00:05:19,000
archaeologists found the oldest inscribed papyri ever discovered, including the diary of an inspector named Merer.

40
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:27,400
It records his team ferrying limestone from the quarries at Tura to a harbour at Giza, trip after trip, in the reign of Khufu.

41
00:05:27,400 --> 00:05:36,600
A pyramid wasn't just a tomb. Each came with temples, a causeway and farmland whose harvests paid priests to make offerings to the dead king forever.

42
00:05:36,600 --> 00:05:42,800
Over generations, more and more of Egypt's land and grain went to supporting dead kings and their priests.

43
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:49,700
Khufu's son Khafre built the second pyramid at Giza, and the Great Sphinx was most likely carved in his reign.

44
00:05:49,700 --> 00:05:57,600
The third, built by Menkaure, is far smaller. After Giza, pyramids kept shrinking. That tells you something. The kings were spending less,

45
00:05:57,600 --> 00:06:04,800
while the officials, priests and provincial governors who ran the state were growing richer and more independent.

46
00:06:04,800 --> 00:06:11,700
Late in this era, kings began carving spells on their burial chamber walls to help them rise to the sky after death.

47
00:06:11,700 --> 00:06:20,400
These Pyramid Texts are among the oldest religious writings in the world. For about 500 years, Egypt's god-kings built mountains of stone.

48
00:06:20,400 --> 00:06:28,300
Then the river stopped cooperating. Around 2200 BC, the climate shifted and the floods shrank, year after year.

49
00:06:28,300 --> 00:06:35,500
Harvests failed and people starved. And a god-king who couldn't make the river rise no longer looked much like a god.

50
00:06:35,500 --> 00:06:41,700
Pepi II, the last great king of this era, may have reigned for more than 60 years. By then,

51
00:06:41,700 --> 00:06:50,400
provincial governors had made their posts hereditary and were ruling like independent kings. After him, central power collapsed.

52
00:06:50,400 --> 00:07:00,100
For more than a century, there was no single Egypt. Rival dynasties ruled from the north and from Thebes in the south, local strongmen fought each other,

53
00:07:00,100 --> 00:07:09,800
and famine hit village after village. Historians call it the First Intermediate Period. Something lasting came out of it, too. With the kings weakened,

54
00:07:09,800 --> 00:07:18,700
wealthy local families began claiming an afterlife that had once belonged to the king, painting the old royal spells inside their own coffins.

55
00:07:18,700 --> 00:07:27,100
Around 2050 BC, Mentuhotep II of Thebes defeated the northern dynasty and reunited the country.

56
00:07:27,100 --> 00:07:33,300
This began the Middle Kingdom. Its kings had learned from the collapse. They moved the capital north,

57
00:07:33,300 --> 00:07:39,800
near the Faiyum oasis, drained its marshes to grow more food, cut the power of the provincial governors,

58
00:07:39,800 --> 00:07:48,200
and built a chain of massive forts along the Nile in Nubia to guard the south. For about 300 years, Egypt was stable again.

59
00:07:48,200 --> 00:07:57,400
It was also Egypt's classic age of literature. Stories and teachings written in this period were copied in schools for centuries afterwards.

60
00:07:57,400 --> 00:08:06,600
But after about two centuries, the royal line weakened. Dozens of short-lived kings followed one another, and their grip on the north slipped.

61
00:08:06,600 --> 00:08:16,000
Meanwhile, settlers from the Levant had been moving into the northern delta. As the Middle Kingdom weakened, their leaders took control of the north,

62
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:24,900
around 1650 BC, ruling from their capital, Avaris. Egyptians called them "rulers of foreign lands." We call them the Hyksos.

63
00:08:24,900 --> 00:08:32,300
They had horse-drawn chariots, powerful composite bows and better bronze weapons, which Egypt had barely used before.

64
00:08:32,300 --> 00:08:39,500
For the first time, foreigners ruled the land of the pharaohs, and the Egyptian kings in Thebes had to accept them.

65
00:08:39,500 --> 00:08:48,900
Yet the Hyksos didn't try to erase Egypt. They took pharaohs' titles, wrote their names in hieroglyphs, and worshipped Egyptian gods alongside their own.

66
00:08:48,900 --> 00:08:56,800
It's the first time we see a pattern that runs through this whole story: foreign rulers turning themselves into Egyptian kings.

67
00:08:56,800 --> 00:09:03,300
The kings of Thebes fought back. Seqenenre died in battle, and his mummy's skull still shows the axe and spear wounds.

68
00:09:03,300 --> 00:09:10,000
Thebes was squeezed from both sides, because the Hyksos were in contact with the kingdom of Kush to the south.

69
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:17,900
The next Theban king, Kamose, intercepted a Hyksos letter urging Kush to attack Thebes while his army was away.

70
00:09:17,900 --> 00:09:25,600
The Thebans adopted the enemy's chariots and bows, and around 1550 BC, Ahmose captured Avaris,

71
00:09:25,600 --> 00:09:33,300
drove the Hyksos out, and chased them into Canaan. Foreign rule changed Egypt. It now had a professional army,

72
00:09:33,300 --> 00:09:41,000
and it had learned that the deserts were no longer enough. From now on, it would push its borders outward and build an empire.

73
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:48,900
Ahmose's victory began the New Kingdom, Egypt's richest era. Thebes became its religious heart, and its god Amun,

74
00:09:48,900 --> 00:09:57,300
merged with the sun god Ra, became king of the gods. As the empire grew, his temple at Karnak received a large share of the plunder,

75
00:09:57,300 --> 00:10:06,000
and his priests became enormously rich. The new pharaohs went on the attack. Thutmose I led his army all the way to the Euphrates,

76
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:12,500
in Syria, and deep into Nubia in the south. Almost every pyramid had been robbed by now,

77
00:10:12,500 --> 00:10:19,900
so the new pharaohs hid their tombs instead, cutting them deep into a desert valley near Thebes: the Valley of the Kings.

78
00:10:19,900 --> 00:10:28,300
Around 1479 BC, the child Thutmose III became king, and his aunt and stepmother, Hatshepsut, ruled as regent.

79
00:10:28,300 --> 00:10:37,500
Within a few years, she declared herself pharaoh, and her official images showed her with a king's headdress and the ceremonial false beard.

80
00:10:37,500 --> 00:10:44,700
To justify her rule, she had her temple walls carved with scenes showing the god Amun as her real father.

81
00:10:44,700 --> 00:10:54,100
She ruled for about 20 years, mostly through trade and building rather than war. Her expedition to Punt, on Africa's Red Sea coast, brought back incense,

82
00:10:54,100 --> 00:11:03,300
ebony and living myrrh trees, and her terraced temple at Deir el-Bahari still stands. She also raised giant granite obelisks at Karnak.

83
00:11:03,300 --> 00:11:11,500
Years after her death, her statues were smashed and her name was chiselled off monuments, most likely on Thutmose III's orders.

84
00:11:11,500 --> 00:11:19,700
Later official king lists left her out entirely. Once he ruled alone, Thutmose III became Egypt's greatest conqueror.

85
00:11:19,700 --> 00:11:29,400
Around 1457 BC, rebel kings gathered at Megiddo, in what is now northern Israel. Thutmose took the narrowest of three routes,

86
00:11:29,400 --> 00:11:37,100
a pass so tight his army marched in single file, and caught them by surprise. After a seven-month siege, Megiddo fell.

87
00:11:37,100 --> 00:11:45,300
We know these details because Thutmose had his campaigns carved on the walls of Karnak, listing battles, captured cities and tribute,

88
00:11:45,300 --> 00:11:53,000
much of it given to Amun's temple. Over about 17 campaigns, he built the largest empire Egypt ever had,

89
00:11:53,000 --> 00:12:01,200
stretching from Syria to deep into Sudan, with Nubia's gold mines under Egyptian control. He also found a way to hold it.

90
00:12:01,200 --> 00:12:09,400
Sons of conquered rulers were brought to Egypt and raised at court. When they went home to rule, they had grown up Egyptian, and loyal.

91
00:12:09,400 --> 00:12:18,300
By the reign of Amenhotep III, Egypt was a superpower, rich enough to spend almost four decades building rather than fighting.

92
00:12:18,300 --> 00:12:28,700
Clay tablets later found at Amarna preserve letters from the kings of Babylon and other great powers, writing to the pharaoh as equals and asking him for gold.

93
00:12:28,700 --> 00:12:36,600
Then his son broke the system. Amenhotep IV pushed aside Egypt's many gods for one, the Aten, the disc of the sun,

94
00:12:36,600 --> 00:12:45,000
and renamed himself Akhenaten. Many historians see this partly as a move against the rich and powerful priests of Amun.

95
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:52,400
He built a new capital, now called Amarna, closed the old temples, and had Amun's name hacked off monuments.

96
00:12:52,400 --> 00:13:00,600
Only the king and his queen, Nefertiti, were shown worshipping the Aten directly. Everyone else was meant to worship through them.

97
00:13:00,600 --> 00:13:07,800
Art changed too. The royal family was shown with long faces and rounded bodies, in relaxed family scenes,

98
00:13:07,800 --> 00:13:15,000
a sharp break from 1,500 years of stiff, idealised royal images. Meanwhile, the empire drifted.

99
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:23,200
Among the Amarna letters are pleas from Egypt's vassal rulers in Canaan, begging the pharaoh for troops against their enemies.

100
00:13:23,200 --> 00:13:32,900
Some seem to have gone unanswered. His revolution didn't survive him. After his death, Amarna was abandoned, and Egypt went back to its old gods.

101
00:13:32,900 --> 00:13:42,800
The boy who soon took the throne, Tutankhaten, was about nine. His advisers reopened the old temples and changed his name to honour Amun: Tutankhamun.

102
00:13:42,800 --> 00:13:51,700
He died at around 19, and the exact cause is still debated. A few years later, the army commander Horemheb became pharaoh.

103
00:13:51,700 --> 00:14:00,400
He tore down Akhenaten's temples, reused their blocks, and wiped the Amarna kings, Tutankhamun included, from the official record.

104
00:14:00,400 --> 00:14:07,800
That's partly why Tutankhamun was forgotten. He had been buried in a small, hastily prepared tomb, and later,

105
00:14:07,800 --> 00:14:17,200
workers digging a nearby tomb piled their debris over its entrance. Unlike almost every other royal tomb, it was never emptied by robbers.

106
00:14:17,200 --> 00:14:24,900
In November 1922, British archaeologist Howard Carter uncovered its steps and sealed doorway.

107
00:14:24,900 --> 00:14:32,100
When his sponsor, Lord Carnarvon, asked if he could see anything inside, Carter answered: "Yes, wonderful things."

108
00:14:32,100 --> 00:14:39,000
Inside were more than 5,000 objects. The king lay in three nested coffins, the innermost made of solid gold,

109
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:46,700
about 110 kilograms, with the famous gold mask over his face. And this was the burial of a minor king,

110
00:14:46,700 --> 00:14:54,100
which tells you how much was lost from the others. The discovery made Tutankhamun the most famous pharaoh in the world.

111
00:14:54,100 --> 00:15:03,000
It came in the same year Egypt won formal independence from Britain, and Egyptians embraced the boy king as a symbol of their nation.

112
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:10,900
The treasures stayed in Egypt. Horemheb had no heir, so he passed the throne to another general, Ramesses I.

113
00:15:10,900 --> 00:15:17,600
His family, the Ramessides, were soldiers from the delta, and they set out to rebuild Egypt's empire.

114
00:15:17,600 --> 00:15:25,000
The most famous was Ramesses II, who reigned for 66 years. He built a new capital, Pi-Ramesses,

115
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:32,400
in the eastern delta, closer to the frontier in Asia, and filled Egypt with temples and giant statues of himself,

116
00:15:32,400 --> 00:15:40,600
including the four colossal figures at Abu Simbel. Later Egyptians remembered him as the greatest king of all,

117
00:15:40,600 --> 00:15:48,800
and nine later pharaohs took his name. In 1274 BC, he fought the Hittite empire for control of Syria at Kadesh,

118
00:15:48,800 --> 00:15:56,200
in one of the largest chariot battles in history. Ramesses was caught in an ambush and had to fight his way out.

119
00:15:56,200 --> 00:16:04,900
The battle ended without a clear winner, though Ramesses advertised it at home as a great victory. About 15 years later,

120
00:16:04,900 --> 00:16:13,100
the two empires signed one of the earliest peace treaties whose text survives, promising to help each other against attackers.

121
00:16:13,100 --> 00:16:20,500
Ramesses later married a Hittite princess to seal the peace. Around 1200 BC, the Bronze Age world collapsed.

122
00:16:20,500 --> 00:16:27,700
The Hittite empire fell, and cities across the eastern Mediterranean were destroyed. Under Ramesses III,

123
00:16:27,700 --> 00:16:37,100
Egypt beat back invaders it called the Sea Peoples, but war, failing trade and poor harvests drained it. Egypt soon lost its lands in Canaan and Syria.

124
00:16:37,100 --> 00:16:46,800
The state began to fail at home. Around 1157 BC, royal tomb builders went unpaid and staged the first recorded strike in history.

125
00:16:46,800 --> 00:16:53,500
Within decades, officials were putting gangs on trial for robbing the royal tombs in the Valley of the Kings.

126
00:16:53,500 --> 00:17:00,200
To save what was left, priests gathered the mummies of great pharaohs and hid them together in secret caches.

127
00:17:00,200 --> 00:17:09,100
That's why we can still see the faces of kings like Ramesses II today. By about 1070 BC, the New Kingdom was over.

128
00:17:09,100 --> 00:17:17,000
The high priests of Amun ruled the south from Thebes, while separate kings ruled the delta. Power slipped to outsiders.

129
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:25,200
Families of Libyan descent, settled in Egypt for generations, took the throne. Then kings from Kush, in today's Sudan, marched north.

130
00:17:25,200 --> 00:17:34,600
Around 727 BC, King Piye conquered Egypt, and his family ruled as pharaohs for more than half a century,

131
00:17:34,600 --> 00:17:41,800
restoring old temples and traditions. Next came the Assyrian empire. In 671 BC,

132
00:17:41,800 --> 00:17:49,700
its army took Memphis, and in 663 BC it sacked Thebes and drove the Kushite kings back south for good.

133
00:17:49,700 --> 00:17:56,400
A delta prince, Psamtik I, used the Assyrians' retreat and hired Greek soldiers to reunite Egypt.

134
00:17:56,400 --> 00:18:02,900
His dynasty was the last great native revival, and it lasted about 140 years.

135
00:18:02,900 --> 00:18:09,100
It opened Egypt to Greek traders, and Psamtik's son Necho II tried to win back Syria.

136
00:18:09,100 --> 00:18:19,500
But in 605 BC, the Babylonian prince Nebuchadnezzar crushed the Egyptian army at Carchemish. Egypt's days as an empire were over.

137
00:18:19,500 --> 00:18:27,400
In 525 BC, the Persian king Cambyses defeated the last king of that dynasty at Pelusium,

138
00:18:27,400 --> 00:18:34,800
on Egypt's eastern border. Egypt became a province of the largest empire the world had yet seen, ruled from Iran.

139
00:18:34,800 --> 00:18:41,300
Some Persian kings, like Darius I, respected Egyptian temples and ruled through local officials.

140
00:18:41,300 --> 00:18:49,000
Others were harsher, and Egyptians rebelled again and again. In 404 BC, they finally broke free.

141
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:58,200
Independence lasted about 60 years. In 343 BC, Persia invaded again and won. The pharaoh who lost that war,

142
00:18:58,200 --> 00:19:07,100
Nectanebo II, is usually counted as the last native Egyptian to rule Egypt for about 2,300 years.

143
00:19:07,100 --> 00:19:15,500
That's the number from the start of this video. In 332 BC, Alexander the Great took Egypt from the Persians,

144
00:19:15,500 --> 00:19:23,200
and many Egyptians welcomed him. He travelled to the oracle of Amun at Siwa, in the western desert,

145
00:19:23,200 --> 00:19:30,900
and was hailed as the god's son. Egypt accepted him as pharaoh. He founded a new city on the coast: Alexandria.

146
00:19:30,900 --> 00:19:37,800
He left after a few months and never returned alive, but his body was later brought back and buried there.

147
00:19:37,800 --> 00:19:44,500
After Alexander's death in 323 BC, his general Ptolemy took Egypt,

148
00:19:44,500 --> 00:19:52,400
and Ptolemy's family ruled it for nearly 300 years. The system was simple: Greek-speaking officials at the top,

149
00:19:52,400 --> 00:20:01,800
Egyptian farmers at the bottom, and the Nile's harvest taxed to fund the court. To Egyptians, the Ptolemies presented themselves as pharaohs.

150
00:20:01,800 --> 00:20:10,000
They built huge temples in the traditional style, like those at Edfu and Dendera, with their Greek names carved in hieroglyphs.

151
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:19,200
Foreign rulers kept becoming, at least on temple walls, Egyptian kings. Alexandria became the greatest city of the Greek world, with its lighthouse,

152
00:20:19,200 --> 00:20:27,600
one of the Seven Wonders, and its Great Library. Scholars there worked out the size of the Earth and wrote the geometry still taught today.

153
00:20:27,600 --> 00:20:34,800
But the royal court itself spoke Greek. The Ptolemies kept power in the family, often by brothers marrying sisters.

154
00:20:34,800 --> 00:20:43,500
Heavy taxes bred resentment, and for about 20 years, rebels held much of southern Egypt. In 196 BC,

155
00:20:43,500 --> 00:20:52,200
during that revolt, priests at Memphis issued a decree honouring the young king Ptolemy V. It was carved in three scripts: hieroglyphs,

156
00:20:52,200 --> 00:21:01,400
the everyday Egyptian script called Demotic, and Greek. Remember this stone. The last ruler of the dynasty was Cleopatra VII.

157
00:21:01,400 --> 00:21:07,600
According to the ancient writer Plutarch, she was the first of her family to learn Egyptian.

158
00:21:07,600 --> 00:21:15,500
She needed powerful allies to keep Egypt free of Rome, which was swallowing the Mediterranean. In 48 BC,

159
00:21:15,500 --> 00:21:24,400
she was fighting her own brother for the throne when Julius Caesar arrived in Alexandria. She won his support, he defeated her rivals,

160
00:21:24,400 --> 00:21:33,100
and she had a son with him, Caesarion. After Caesar's murder, she allied with Mark Antony, who controlled Rome's eastern provinces.

161
00:21:33,100 --> 00:21:43,000
That made them enemies of Caesar's heir, Octavian. When Antony and Cleopatra lost the naval battle of Actium to Octavian in 31 BC,

162
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:50,400
it was over. Antony killed himself, and in 30 BC Cleopatra died too, by tradition from a snake bite.

163
00:21:50,400 --> 00:21:57,600
Egypt became part of the Roman Empire. Octavian, now Emperor Augustus, kept Egypt as his personal
